%I A399810 #38 Oct 15 2026 09:40:25
%S A399810 0,1,3,6,21,40,81,108,383,417,1067,1686,4506,7842,19746,55312
%N A399810 Numbers k such that 42*10^(k+2) + 43 is prime.
%C A399810 42*10^(k+2) + 43 is the decimal concatenation "42", a run of k zeros, and "43"; it has k + 4 digits.
%C A399810 The digit sum is 13 for every k, so the number is always 1 mod 3; the number is also never divisible by 7 or 11, for any k.
%C A399810 a(6) = 40 corresponds to 42*10^42 + 43, the only known prime of the form n*10^n + n + 1 (with n = 42); see Earls link.
%H A399810 Jason Earls, <a href="https://t5k.org/curios/page.php?number_id=5841">42000...00043 (44-digits)</a>, Prime Curios! (The Prime Pages).
%e A399810 0 is a term since 42*10^2 + 43 = 4243, prime.
%e A399810 1 is a term since 42*10^3 + 43 = 42043, prime.
%e A399810 2 is not a term since 42*10^4 + 43 = 420043 = 13 * 32311, not prime.
%e A399810 3 is a term since 42*10^5 + 43 = 4200043, prime.
%o A399810 (Python)
%o A399810 import gmpy2
%o A399810 print([k for k in range(201) if gmpy2.is_prime(42*10**(k+2) + 43, 25)])
%Y A399810 Cf. A399161 (corresponding primes).
%K A399810 nonn,hard,more
%O A399810 1,3
%A A399810 _Anthony Bean_, Oct 11 2026
%E A399810 a(16) from _Michael S. Branicky_, Oct 13 2026
